onblack('marsh', 'froth', "Froth on dark brine, about 1.5 metres across: clusters of pink-white bubbles of many sizes, rafts of pale scum, thin strings of foam and a few larger glassy domes, with empty black between them.", 1, 'plate-norris-2.jpg', 'the froth and small bubbles of the seep pool', 1.5, asked=MG, group=3)
ground('marsh', 'shore', "The bank of a pool of blood-red brine, a strip 4 metres wide and 2.25 metres tall whose bands run level from the left edge to the right edge. Along the bottom edge is open dark red brine; above it a thin line of pale scum; then a band of dark wet red mud; then a band of cream-white salt in lumps like cauliflower, with red in every crevice; and along the top edge drier grey salt crust. The bands keep the same height across the whole width.", 1, 'plate-porcelain-1.jpg', 'the bank of the seep pool and the channels; repeat it left to right with every second copy mirrored', 4, asked=MG, group=2)
sheet('marsh', 'wallower', "One mud-wallower, an animal of an unknown world, standing, seen exactly from the side in an orthographic view, facing left, about 2.6 metres tall and 5 metres long. A huge heavy barrel of a body on four short thick legs, under thick grey-tan hide in deep folds and coarse wrinkles with sparse short bristles, caked to the belly in drying red-brown mud. It has no neck and no head: the front of the body slopes down to the ground and ends in a fringe of eight or ten long curved pale feelers like tusks or the claws of a sloth, which rake the mud.", 3, 'ref-hide-2.jpg', 'the wallower whole in side view, for the shape of its one skin', 5, animal=True, group=1)
sheet('marsh', 'stalk', "One stalk of a stalk colony, a growth of an unknown world, whole, seen square on, about 3 metres tall, standing upright with its foot at the bottom of the frame. A single thick stem, slightly bent, sheathed in overlapping pale cream and bone-white fibrous rings crusted with salt, each ring with a ragged dry brown lower edge. From its top springs a crown of long crimson fans: flat ribbed blades that split into hundreds of fine wet red filaments, some upright and some hanging, darker wine red at the base.", 2, 'plate-forest-1.jpg', 'one stalk with its fan, for the filaments', 3, aspect='9:16', group=1)
sheet('marsh', 'lock', "One lock of the fan of a stalk, a growth of an unknown world, laid flat and seen square on, 0.7 metres long, its base at the bottom of the frame: 25 to 40 fine wet crimson filaments fanning upward and outward from one flat pale strap-like base, each filament thin as string, darker wine red near the base and brighter at the ragged tips, a few clear drops of water hanging on them.", 2, 'ref-tissue-1.jpg', 'one lock of a fan, for the filaments of the stalks', 0.7, aspect='1:1', asked=ML, group=2)
sheet('marsh', 'deadone', "The carcass of a mud-wallower, an animal of an unknown world, long dead and crusted with salt, seen from straight above, 3 metres long: a collapsed sunken hide lying flat, covered in a thick crust of white and pale grey salt crystals in lumps and knobs, with the curved ribs and the short thick leg bones standing through the crust as pale spokes, and dirty pink-brown stain in the hollows.", 4, 'plate-porcelain-1.jpg', 'one of the old dead under their salt', 3, height=True, animal=True, asked=ML, group=2)
tile('marsh', 'wethide', "A material texture, seen straight on: the soaked hide of a huge heavy wallowing animal below the line where it stands in red brine, about 1 metre across. Dark grey-brown skin in deep folds and coarse wrinkles, soaking wet, smeared with glossy rust-red mud that fills the folds, with thin runs of red water.", 3, 'ref-hide-2.jpg', 'the wet hide below the wet line', 1, asked=ML, group=3)
sheet('marsh', 'insect', "One small flying insect of an unknown world with its wings spread, seen from straight above, enlarged to fill the frame: a slender dark segmented body 2 centimetres long that ends at the front in a tuft of fine feelers, with no eyes, and two pairs of long clear glassy wings with a fine net of dark veins, the front pair longer than the back pair.", 2, 'ref-tissue-1.jpg', 'one of the insects round the fans', 0.04, aspect='1:1', asked=ML, group=3)
add('marsh', 'muzzle', 'plate', "The front end of a mud-wallower, an animal of an unknown world, seen exactly from the front, 1.2 metres wide: a blunt heavy wrinkled muzzle of thick grey-tan hide, with a slack lipless slit of a mouth low down and, hanging round and below it, a fringe of a dozen long pale curved feelers like tusks, caked with drying red-brown mud. It has no eyes, no ears and no nostrils: above the mouth there is only folded hide. It is not a monster and not cute. " + SHEET, 4, 'ref-hide-2.jpg', 'the front of a wallower, as the stuck one shows it', 1.2, '1:1', H_SHEET, asked=ML, group=2)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The Spine
SG, SS = 'spine_ground_owner_2', 'spine_storm_owner_2'
back('spine', 'far', "A photograph taken at dusk with a long lens from a high knife ridge above a sea of cloud, looking along the ridge. Only the far distance is in the picture, everything further than about three kilometres: the ridge runs away from the left of the frame toward the right as a chain of separate rock spires, each a leaning blade of blue-grey rock with snow in its gullies and a tip burned black, each smaller, bluer and fainter than the one before, the last ones only shapes in the haze. Storm cloud stands against the left side of the ridge and pours over the low saddles between the spires in long falling veils. A sea of cloud lies far below on the right, its tops rolling, grey-blue and mauve. Above, the storm is dark blue-grey and violet with torn undersides; along the horizon at the right lies one narrow band of clear sky, orange and pale yellow. No lightning. The horizon lies a little below the middle of the frame.", 0, 'plate-storm-1.jpg', 'the far spires, the storm and the cloud sea beyond the built ridge', group=1)
sheet('spine', 'tower', "One whole spire of the ridge, seen square on from the side in an orthographic view: a mountain of blue-grey and tan rock that is the weathered bone of a colossal vertebra, 500 metres tall, with its foot at the bottom of the frame. It rises as a steep broken wall and leans over to the left as a great blade whose tip is burned black and glassy. Its face is split by long joints into slabs and flakes with sharp edges, snow lies in every gully and on every ledge, a round dark tunnel mouth opens low in its side, and broken rock and snow fans lie at its foot.", 0, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'one spire whole, for the shape of the leaning blade and its broken face', 500, aspect='9:16', height=True, group=1)
sheet('spine', 'runner', "One ridge-runner, an animal of an unknown world, standing, seen exactly from the side in an orthographic view, facing right, about 1.8 metres tall at the shoulder. A heavy four-legged animal of the high cold, with a humped shoulder and thick legs that end in broad dark paws with blunt claws, under a long shaggy coat of dirty cream and pale grey hair that hangs in matted locks almost to the ground, with ice frozen into the tips. Where a face would be, the coat parts round a bunch of short dark leathery tendrils that hang down.", 4, 'ref-dusk-1.jpg', 'the ridge-runner whole in side view, for the shape under the coat', 2.6, animal=True, group=1)
sheet('spine', 'lock', "Eight separate hanging locks of long matted fur side by side in one row, each with its root at the top and ending in a point at the bottom, each about 35 centimetres long and 6 to 10 centimetres wide, each different, with black between them. Cream-white and pale grey coarse wet hair, clumped and twisted, darker at the root, with beads of rime ice on the tips.", 4, 'ref-dusk-1.jpg', 'locks of the coat, for hair cards', 0.35, aspect='1:1', height=True, asked=SS, group=2)
ground('spine', 'scorchfield', "The place on a high snowy ridge where lightning came to ground, a piece 14 metres wide and 7.9 metres tall. In the middle, about 4 metres across, bare rock burned black and glassy, with fused branching channels like roots spreading outward from one point; round it a ring where the snow has melted back and refrozen as clear wet ice over dark rock; outside the ring cold blue-white wind-packed snow with sharp wind ridges that all run one way and a few dark stones.", 1, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'the ground where the bolt came down, whole', 14, asked=SG + ' and ' + SS, group=2)
sheet('spine', 'burn', "A piece of the coat and hide of a large shaggy animal, laid flat and seen square on, about 1 metre wide. Long cream-white matted fur round the edges; toward the middle the fur is singed to short black curled stubs; in the middle one long raw split in the skin, red-black and dry, with charred edges.", 1, 'ref-dusk-1.jpg', 'the burn on the scorched one', 1, aspect='1:1', asked=SS, group=3)
sheet('spine', 'membrane', "One ribbon of a tether-kite, a growth of an unknown world, laid flat and seen square on: 14 metres long and 1.5 metres at its widest, lying level across the frame, widest near its stalk at the left and tapering to the right. A translucent skin like the skin of a fish or a jellyfish, grey-white with a faint violet sheen, with fine branching veins, dark frayed edges, one tear half way through it, and a ragged torn end.", 2, 'ref-tissue-1.jpg', 'one kite ribbon, for the membrane of the kites', 14, height=True, asked=SS, group=2)
sheet('spine', 'holdfast', "The holdfast of a tether-kite, a growth of an unknown world, seen square on, about 1.5 metres across: a cluster of dark leathery knobs and bulbs like the holdfast of kelp, packed together and crusted with white rime, with several thin dark stalks leaving it toward the right.", 2, 'ref-tissue-1.jpg', 'the holdfasts of the kites in the clefts', 1.5, aspect='1:1', height=True, asked=SS, group=3)
tile('spine', 'tendril', "A material texture, seen straight on: the skin of a dark leathery tendril, about 0.3 metres across. Dark grey-brown wet leathery skin with fine cross wrinkles and small bumps, a slight sheen, and grains of white rime frost in the creases. No hair.", 4, 'ref-hide-2.jpg', 'the skin of the tendrils of the faces', 0.3, asked=SS, group=3)
sheet('spine', 'stump', "One whole spire of a high ridge, seen square on from the side in an orthographic view: a snapped stump of blue-grey and tan rock that is the weathered bone of a colossal vertebra, 250 metres tall, with its foot at the bottom of the frame. Its upper half has broken away long ago: the top is split into two jagged prongs, burned black at their tips, and one whole shoulder has fallen and lies as a slope of huge blocks against its side. The face is split by long joints into slabs and flakes with sharp edges, with snow in every gully and on every ledge.", 3, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'a second spire, unlike the first: a snapped stump', 250, aspect='9:16', height=True, asked=SG, group=2)
sheet('spine', 'horn', "One whole spire of a high ridge, seen square on from the side in an orthographic view: a slender horn of blue-grey and tan rock that is the weathered bone of a colossal vertebra, 250 metres tall, with its foot at the bottom of the frame. It rises thin and steep from a narrow base and hooks over to the right at the top like a thorn, its tip burned black and glassy. The face is split by long joints that follow its lean into thin slabs and flakes with sharp edges, with thin lines of snow in the cracks and a small snow fan at its foot.", 3, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'a third spire, unlike the first: a slender hooked horn', 250, aspect='9:16', height=True, asked=SG, group=2)
add('spine', 'stormwall', 'plate', "A photograph of storm cloud only, at dusk, seen from a high mountain crest looking into the wind: the windward wall of a thunderstorm standing close, heaped towers of dark blue-grey and violet cloud one above another, darkest beneath, their edges torn sideways by a gale, with grey veils of falling snow hanging under the lowest bellies and paler mauve where the cloud thins high up. Only cloud is in the frame: no land, no ridge, no horizon, no band of clear sky, no lightning, no sun. The first reference image is the place this storm belongs to: take its colour and its light from there. The second reference image is a real photograph: it is the standard for how a camera records a real storm, with real soft gradients and real cloud structure and no painted or stylised look. Output one wide landscape image, sixteen units wide and nine tall. Photographic, no text, no logo, no people, no birds.", 0, 'plate-storm-1.jpg', 'the storm wall, as a picture sky that can flow', 0, '16:9', None, asked=SG, group=2)
ground('spine', 'icefloor', "The frozen floor of a tunnel through rock, a strip 8 metres wide and 14 metres long that runs from the top edge of the frame to the bottom edge. Dark clear ice with white cracks and frozen ripples down the middle, snow drifted in along both the left and the right edge, a few chunks of broken ice and small dark stones frozen in. The strip looks the same in character at the top and at the bottom, so that it can be repeated along its length.", 4, 'plate-tube-1.jpg', 'the floor of the canal; repeat it along its length with every second copy mirrored', 8, aspect='9:16', asked=SG, group=2)
tile('spine', 'ledge', "A material texture, seen square on in an orthographic view: the flank of a high mountain ridge, a section about 40 metres tall. Dark blue-grey rock in level beds of unequal thickness, broken into blocks and short walls, with snow lying on every ledge and thin snow in the cracks. The beds run exactly horizontal.", 3, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'the bedded flanks below the spires', 40, asked=SG, group=3)
tile('spine', 'rime', "A material texture, seen square on in an orthographic view: rime ice on pale rock, about 3 metres across. White feathers and plates of rime grown into the wind, all pointing one way from right to left, thick on every edge and thin in the hollows, with pale tan-grey rock showing between them.", 2, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'the rime on the first spire', 3, asked=SG, group=3)
# Two pictures made from a picture of this list. `stormwall-lit` is `stormwall` passed back as the one reference. `scorch` is the middle of `scorchfield`
# (its middle 6 of 14 metres, cut and enlarged by `python3 assets-src/materials/cut.py assets-src/spine/mat/src/scorchfield.png assets-src/spine/mat/src/scorch-in.png 0.5 0.5 0.4286`), made again at full resolution.
add('spine', 'stormwall-lit', 'plate', "The reference image is a photograph of the wall of a thunderstorm at dusk. Make the same photograph, with every cloud form exactly where it is, at the instant that lightning flashes deep inside the cloud: the towers glow from within, violet-white, brightest in two or three places in the heart of the cloud and falling off softly through it, and the edges of the nearer towers stand dark against the glow. No lightning channel and no bolt is visible, only the glow inside the cloud. Nothing else changes: the same framing and the same cloud shapes, pixel for pixel. Photographic, no text, no logo.", 0, 'plate-storm-1.jpg', 'the same storm wall lit from inside by a strike, to blend with the first by each strike', 0, '16:9', None, asked=SG, group=4, refs=['assets-src/spine/mat/src/stormwall.png'])
add('spine', 'scorch', 'plate', "The first reference image is a blurred, low-resolution survey photograph, seen straight down from above, of a piece of ground 6 metres wide and 3.4 metres tall on a high snowy ridge, where lightning came to ground: bare rock burned black and glassy in the middle, with fused branching channels like roots spreading from one point, a ring of refrozen clear ice round it and wind-packed snow outside. Make the same photograph at full resolution. Keep every form exactly where it is and at the same size: add nothing new at this scale and move nothing. Add what a real camera records at this scale and the first image lacks: the glassy shine, bubbles and fine cracks of fused rock, the raised glazed rims of the channels, grit and small burst flakes of stone, clear ice with white cracks and trapped bubbles over dark rock, and the grain and sharp wind edges of packed snow. Flat, even, shadowless overcast light. The second reference image is a real photograph: it is the standard for how a camera records real rock, ice and snow, with real grain and micro detail and no painted or stylised look. Photographic, sharp from edge to edge, no text, no logo, no people, no animals.", 0, 'plate-ridge-1.jpg', 'the middle of the scorched ground close, for the detail of the scorched one; it lies on the middle 6 metres of `scorchfield`', 6, '16:9', H_GROUND, asked=SS, group=4, refs=['assets-src/spine/mat/src/scorch-in.png', R + 'plate-ridge-1.jpg'])


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The Eye
EO = 'eye_owner_2'
back('eye', 'far', "A photograph taken on a clear, windless evening from a slope above the shore of a round mountain lake that lies in a socket of cliffs, looking across the lake. Only the far side is in the picture, everything beyond the middle of the lake: a long curving wall of pale limestone cliffs about 200 metres tall and two kilometres away runs the whole width of the frame, in thick level beds of cream, buff and gold, broken into terraces and buttresses, with caves and dark alcoves along some beds and fans of pale scree at its foot. The low sun from the left lights the left and middle of the wall warm gold; toward the right the wall turns into cool blue shade and rises into a great overhanging brow of rock. A thin line of mist lies at the foot of the cliffs along the far shore. The sky is cloudless: pale gold low down, deepening to soft blue. The top of the cliffs lies one third of the way down the frame and the far shore line lies at the middle of the frame. Below the shore line there is only still dark blue water that mirrors the cliffs: no near shore, no rocks, no plants.", 0, 'plate-crater-1.jpg', 'the far rim of the socket, sunlit, for the establishing frame', group=1)
ground('eye', 'screeplate', "A slope of scree above a lake shore, a piece 24 metres wide and 13.5 metres tall. Angular blocks and slabs of pale grey and cream limestone from fist size to 2 metres, piled and interlocked, with gravel and coarse pale sand between them, a few tufts of dry pale growth, and dark gaps under the largest blocks.", 0, 'plate-crater-1.jpg', 'the scree of the near foreground of the establishing picture', 24, group=1)
face('eye', 'face', "The wall of a great basin of pale rock, a face 360 metres wide and 200 metres tall. Massive pale limestone in thick level beds, cream, buff and pale gold, broken into buttresses, ledges and stepped terraces of unequal depth, with rounded overhangs, dark alcoves and shallow caves along some beds, vertical black and rust water stains, sparse dark green growth on the ledges, and steep fans of pale scree along the foot.", 0, 'plate-crater-1.jpg', 'the cliff of the rim, for its beds, caves and scree fans', 360, group=1)
back('eye', 'far-low', "A photograph taken on a clear, windless evening from two metres above the water at the shore of a round mountain lake that lies in a socket of cliffs, looking straight across the lake with a wide lens. Only the far side is in the picture: a wall of pale limestone cliffs about 200 metres tall and 1.6 kilometres away runs the whole width of the frame. On the left and in the middle the low sun from the left lights broken terraces and buttresses warm gold, with caves and dark alcoves; the right flank is in cool blue shade, with fans of rubble at its foot. The sky is cloudless: pale gold low down, deepening to soft blue. No mist. The far shore line lies exactly at the middle of the frame. Below it there is only still dark water: no near shore, no rocks, no plants.", 2, 'plate-crater-1.jpg', 'the north rim as the low cameras of the mats and the edge see it', asked=EO, group=2)
back('eye', 'far-long', "A photograph taken on a clear, windless evening with a very long lens across a mountain lake: the wall of pale limestone cliffs on the far shore, 1.5 kilometres away and 200 metres tall, fills the frame from side to side and from top to bottom. Warm sunlit fractured ledges on the left; dark vertical ribs of rock in the middle; right of centre a recessed cave above a cool shaded slope of scree with sparse green growth. No water, no mist and no sky in the frame.", 4, 'plate-crater-1.jpg', 'the north wall through the long lens of the last detail', asked=EO, group=2)
DR = "One drinker, an animal of an unknown world, about 2 metres tall at the shoulder: a tall lean four-legged animal with long thin legs and small cloven hooves, a deep narrow chest, and a very long neck that tapers to a small blunt smooth muzzle. Its pale tan and sandy fawn hide has very short dense hair and fine creases, slightly darker along faint ridges where the ribs lie beneath. "
sheet('eye', 'drinker-down', DR + "It is seen exactly from the side in an orthographic view, facing right, standing with its forelegs a little apart and its neck lowered so that its muzzle touches the ground in front of it, as if drinking.", 1, 'ref-hide-3.jpg', 'a drinker, neck down, for the far herd', 2.6, height=True, animal=True, asked=EO, group=2)
sheet('eye', 'drinker-up', DR + "It is seen exactly from the side in an orthographic view, facing left, standing square with its neck raised and its muzzle held level, alert.", 1, 'ref-hide-3.jpg', 'a drinker, head up', 2.6, height=True, animal=True, asked=EO, group=2)
sheet('eye', 'drinker-back', DR + "It is seen exactly from behind in an orthographic view, standing square, its rump and hind legs toward the viewer and its long neck raised above its back.", 4, 'ref-hide-3.jpg', 'a drinker from behind, as the last detail sees them', 2.6, aspect='1:1', height=True, animal=True, asked=EO, group=2)
sheet('eye', 'calf', "One calf of the drinkers, animals of an unknown world, about 1 metre tall at the shoulder: a young lean four-legged animal with long thin legs too long for its body, small hooves, and a long neck that tapers to a small blunt smooth muzzle, under pale sandy fawn hide with very short dense hair. It is seen exactly from the side in an orthographic view, facing right, with its neck lowered so that its muzzle touches the ground in front of it.", 1, 'ref-hide-3.jpg', 'a calf, neck down', 1.4, height=True, animal=True, asked=EO, group=2)
ground('eye', 'matplate', "The bed of the very shallow, perfectly clear margin of a lake, a piece 12 metres wide and 12 metres tall, seen with no ripples and no reflections. Long fine filaments of amber, rust and gold grow over it in mats, all combed toward the top of the picture, crossing and clumped in thick ropes in places and thin as hair in others; pale limestone slabs stand through the mats; between them are dark gaps of clear deeper water.", 2, 'plate-terrace-2.jpg', 'the amber mats, with their strands, slabs and gaps', 12, aspect='1:1', asked=EO, group=2)
sheet('eye', 'skeleton', "The skeleton of a tall four-legged animal of an unknown world, lying on its side and seen from straight above, 5 metres long: a rib cage of thin curved ribs, some broken, a long spine of small vertebrae running on into a very long neck that ends in a plain tapering point with no skull, a pelvis, and long thin leg bones folded together. Weathered ivory bone, dry, cracked and pitted, stained amber in places.", 3, 'plate-crater-1.jpg', 'the skeleton on the edge', 5, height=True, asked=EO, group=2)
ground('eye', 'lip', "The lip where the shallow floor of a lake stops at a sheer drop, a piece 10 metres wide and 10 metres tall, seen with no water on it. Wet pale sinter and grey-tan rock in scalloped ledges and thin layers, knobbly and pitted, with amber and rust strands draped over the raised edges and tan stain in the hollows. The drop lies along the top edge of the picture, where the rock ends in a broken overhanging rim and pure black beyond it.", 3, 'plate-terrace-2.jpg', 'the lip where the floor stops', 10, aspect='1:1', asked=EO, group=2)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The Reveal
RO = 'reveal_owner_2'
RV = '7-reveal/reveal-4-titan-from-orbit.jpg'
ORB = ("The sun is high overhead, so the cloud tops are evenly lit and no long shadow falls to one side. The sea beneath is pure black: no land, no coast, no sun glint. The field fills the whole frame edge to edge at one uniform scale, as a seamless tileable texture. "
       "The first reference image is the world this cloud belongs to: take the colour and the softness of its cloud from there, and nothing of its composition. "
       "The second reference image is a real satellite photograph: it is the standard for how a camera in orbit records real cloud, with real soft edges, real lumpy and fibrous detail at every size and no painted or stylised look. "
       "Output one square image. Photographic, sharp, no text, no logo, no border, no grid lines, no stars.")
add('reveal', 'popcorn', 'tile', "Cloud seen straight down from orbit in an orthographic view: a field of small fair-weather cumulus over open ocean, about 250 kilometres across. Thousands of separate small white puffs, each 1 to 4 kilometres across, in loose clumps and short streets with clear black gaps between them, denser in some parts of the field and sparse in others, every puff lumpy with a soft edge. " + ORB, '7-reveal/reveal-2-skull-massif.jpg', 'orb-modis-popcorn.jpg', 'the popcorn field of the lower keys', 250000, '1:1', None, asked=RO, group=2)
add('reveal', 'streaks', 'tile', "Cloud seen straight down from orbit in an orthographic view: the fine wind-drawn streaks and flecks of small cloud that cover a whole sea, about 1,600 kilometres across. Thin, torn white streaks and beaded lines all drawn roughly one way from left to right, with brighter knots, small flecks between them and wide black gaps, never ruled straight and never evenly spaced. " + ORB, RV, 'orb-modis-streets.jpg', 'the wind streaks that cover the whole sea in the widest key', 1600000, '1:1', None, asked=RO, group=2)
add('reveal', 'glitter', 'tile', "A texture seen straight down from orbit in an orthographic view: open sea in sunglint, about 400 kilometres across. A silver-grey sheen over dark blue water, broken by dark smooth slicks in long curved bands, fine parallel swell lines, wind lanes and patches of rougher brighter water. No cloud, no land, no ships. The sea fills the whole frame edge to edge at one uniform scale, as a seamless tileable texture. The first reference image is the world this sea belongs to: take the colour of its sea from there, and nothing of its composition. The second reference image is a real satellite photograph: it is the standard for how a camera in orbit records real sunglint on real water. Output one square image. Photographic, sharp, no text, no logo, no border, no grid lines.", RV, 'orb-iss39-dahlak.jpg', 'the sea in the sun, for a glint that has texture', 400000, '1:1', None, asked=RO, group=2)
add('reveal', 'lakewater', 'tile', "A texture seen straight down from high above in an orthographic view: the surface of a deep lake, about 20 kilometres across. Black-blue water with broad fields of fine wind ripple, darker smooth patches, cat's paws where gusts touch it, and a few faint pale foam lines. No shore, no land, no boats, no cloud. The water fills the whole frame edge to edge at one uniform scale, as a seamless tileable texture. The first reference image is the lake this water belongs to: take its colour from there, and nothing of its composition. The second reference image is a real photograph from orbit: it is the standard for how a camera records real water from above. Output one square image. Photographic, sharp, no text, no logo, no border, no grid lines.", '7-reveal/reveal-1-above-the-lake.jpg', 'orb-iss39-dahlak.jpg', 'the water of the lake in the first frames of the rise', 20000, '1:1', None, asked=RO, group=2)
add('reveal', 'wisps', 'plate', "Vapour seen straight down from orbit in an orthographic view, on a pure black background: three thin translucent trails of white vapour that rise from three points close together near the left edge of the frame and drift to the right for about 500 kilometres, curling, braiding and thinning as they go, each ragged and fibrous, the frame about 600 kilometres wide. Black means nothing is there. Nothing else is in the frame: no land, no sea detail, no cloud field. The first reference image is the world this vapour belongs to: take its softness from there. The second reference image is a real satellite photograph: it is the standard for how a camera in orbit records real thin plumes. Photographic, sharp, no text, no logo, no border.", '7-reveal/reveal-2-skull-massif.jpg', 'orb-modis-sgeorgia-streets.jpg', 'the vapour trails of the throat', 600000, '16:9', None, asked=RO, group=2)

add('spine', 'bolt', 'plate', "A photograph of one lightning stroke at night on a pure black background, taken with a short exposure: a single cloud-to-ground channel that enters at the top centre of the frame and runs down to the bottom centre, crooked and kinked the whole way as real lightning is and never a regular zigzag, thin and blinding white at its core with a narrow violet-blue edge. Eight or ten fine side branches leave it at shallow angles, each fainter and thinner than the channel, forking again and ending in nothing. Black means nothing is there: no glow fog, no cloud, no rain, no ground, no horizon, no stars. The first reference image is the storm this stroke belongs to: take the violet of its lightning from there, and nothing of its composition. The second reference image is a real photograph of a storm: it is the standard for how a camera records the real thing, with no painted, drawn or stylised look. Output one tall portrait image, nine units wide and sixteen tall. Photographic, sharp, no text, no logo, no border, no people.", 3, 'plate-storm-1.jpg', 'the core of the strike, so that the bolt is a photographed channel and not a drawn zigzag; it lies on black, so its brightness is its cover', 340, '9:16', None, asked=SS, group=5)

add('breath', 'carcass-low', 'plate', "The carcass of a wader, an animal of an unknown world, long dead, alone on a pure black background. It is the carcass that lies on the rock in the foreground of the first reference image: make that same animal in the same pose, seen by the same low camera, about one metre above the ground and five metres away, looking only slightly down at it, so that it is seen from the side and a little from above and never from straight above. It lies collapsed on its side, about 4.5 metres from the end of its tendrils to its hooves: the sunken trunk in the middle of the frame, its pale skin split open over the ribs and the hip bones, which stand through it; the long neck lying along the ground to the left and ending in a fan of dull red dried tendrils spread flat; the four long thin legs stretched out to the right and toward the camera, each ending in a small dark hoof. Pale grey-pink dried hide, stained brown where it has split, a little grit lying on it." + BLIND + " The whole carcass lies inside the frame, with a margin of black on every side, and nothing touches the edge of the frame. Black means nothing is there: no ground, no rock, no shadow thrown on any ground, no mist, no plants, no other animal, no horizon, no sky. Soft even light from the front and from above, with no hard shadow and no bright side, so that the film can light it itself. Take the colours, the age and the character of the carcass from the first reference image. " + REAL + NOPE, 2, 'ref-hide-1.jpg', 'the dead one at the rim as the low lens of breath-2 sees it, because a picture taken from straight above cannot carry a view from one metre up', 4.5, '16:9', H_SHEET, asked=BL, group=5)

